    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to wrap up some year-end stuff for our manufacturing plant. Can you walk me through how to do a straight-line depreciation calculation? Like, if we bought a CNC machine for $85,000 and we expect it to have a salvage value of $12,000 after 7 years, how do I calculate the annual depreciation expense? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Straight-line depreciation, a straightforward process, just like a well-organized workshop, where all the tools are neatly labeled and in their place. You know, like a well-run manufacturing plant, where the production line is humming along smoothly, and the workers are as efficient as a well-trained team of... well, not exactly a team of elves, but you get the idea.\n\nNow, let's get to the calculation. To find the annual depreciation expense, you'll need to determine the total depreciable amount, which is the cost of the asset minus its salvage value. In this case, that's $85,000 - $12,000 = $73,000.\n\nNext, you'll divide the total depreciable amount by the asset's useful life in years. In this case, that's 7 years. So, the annual depreciation expense is $73,000 / 7 = $10,429.\n\nThink of it like a treasure chest filled with gold coins, where each year, you get to take out a certain number of coins, representing the depreciation expense. Just like how a clever thief might try to sneak into the chest and grab as many coins as possible, you want to make sure you're calculating the depreciation correctly",
